{"title":"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 9","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>When a pitcher is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> full it contains exactly enough water to fill three identical glasses. How full would the pitcher be if it had exactly enough water to fill four of the same glasses?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/b/93b78a604461f5d6482cce2c804e6f119fe16bfc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{ (A) }\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\qquad\\text{ (B) }\\ \\frac{7}{12} \\qquad\\text{ (C) }\\ \\frac{4}{7} \\qquad\\text{ (D) }\\ \\frac{6}{7} \\qquad\\text{ (E) }\\ \\frac{3}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"417\" height=\"38\" />  \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> pitcher can fill <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> identical glasses, a full pitcher can fill <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/c/aacef164d98ef503fffc8825428c374ce983b52a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\div \\frac{1}{2} = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"80\" height=\"38\" /> identical glasses.\n</p><p>This means that a pitcher that can fill <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> identical glasses must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/7/3c78a56c7634e0570dc3f4bb300a874c15c0bc97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> full. This simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/a/12aa340b91277c3897d2938386d778e9d5f69601.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(A) } \\frac{2}{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"62\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> pitcher can fill <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> identical glasses, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/8/a086090899096d3e90f2a5016b2b555f94f4a7b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} \\div 3 = \\frac{1}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"84\" height=\"38\" /> of a pitcher can fill each glass.\n</p><p>This means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> identical glasses will be filled by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/1/fa1cfe78325bf3dc11211b289ba6b2fe3abb252e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{6} \\times 4 = \\frac{4}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"84\" height=\"38\" /> of a full pitcher. This simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/a/12aa340b91277c3897d2938386d778e9d5f69601.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(A) } \\frac{2}{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"62\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_8\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 8\">Problem 8</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Fol